    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95630.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "Well, the book is about how we shift the food system and how we help farmers kind of convert over and diversify. And obviously they're going to have these very big farms that are going to export a lot of their crop. That's not going to change. But how do we start moving in the direction of regional, sustainable farming where we're asking them and paying them, quite frankly, and I would even argue pay them more than they're making now to supply fresh, more produce to local schools, local prisons, local universities, how do we create real markets for them in areas around the farm and paying them to do it? I think we also were exploring the opportunity to pay them to sequester carbon. I think they need to be a big part of how we move forward with climate. And there are techniques that farmers can use to sequester carbon because you should pay them to do that. And so now you're starting to build out a real economic model in 2019-2021 that reduces the global risks, reduces and maybe insulates them a little bit from the global trade issues that are always presenting themselves. You get them paid. They're part of reversing climate change for us. You move into wind, you know, help continue to move down the road with wind. And also that means a new grid or an updated grid so we can move the power around the country. These are all opportunities that we just have to think differently. And so when I say I'm a New Deal Democrat, I'm not necessarily a New Deal Democrat saying we should do everything that Franklin Roosevelt did. When I say New Deal Democrat, I mean, we should say, what would Franklin Roosevelt do today? What do you think he would do today? I think he would do some of the things we're talking about. You think he'd have a guaranteed jobs",
