    "turn_text": "like, you know, you had people there who, parents, they worked in the steel mills and did a little overtime to pay the tuition. And the tuition wasn't nearly as high as it was as it is now. And so it informed me Because it taught me, I mean, I played sports. I was a quarterback of the team. And we were taught to be leaders. We were taught to not be followers. We would go out into the community. We had senior service where you'd go to nursing homes and you'd put your football jersey on and be a big shot and go to the elementary schools and talk to the kids. The social gospel was very important. I mean, we were, we were Democrats. We were economic Democrats, social Democrats, immigrants. You know, I remember going to family parties where there was broken English spoken, my mom's 100% Italian and, you know, we that we, we were immigrants, you know, my, my great uncles would call, call everybody Americans. He's like, they were the Italians and everyone else was the Americans. And that informed me a lot in And so I went into my public life, pro-life. I mean, I hadn't, in all honesty, hadn't given the abortion issue a whole lot of thought, but I was Catholic school, Catholic person, I was pro-life. And when I got to Congress, so I was pro-life when I was in the States Senate, I got to Congress. I started working with Rosa Deloro, who's a pretty",
