    "turn_idx": 163,
    "turn_text": "But there are kids. And I think I learned that I was married before, too. I had previous marriage, but we didn't have any kids. And what you learn is these relationships are really complicated. Especially when you put kids in the middle of it and the marriage doesn't work out, it gets complicated. You've got to open your heart and you've got to try to work through things and it's not easy. You just look at the country. Everyone's dealing with something. It's not easy. It's very complicated. We've got to stop being so judgmental of everybody. People are just trying to get by. They're trying to make it and I you know talk a lot on the campaign trail about moving from uh survival and surviving to thriving like how do we get to where we're thriving again and and you know what that's what we want for our kids and what that what the last point is uh i start like i don't know if this is like just who i am or whatever but i go to a i go to a classroom of kids and i think like these are all my kids you know like When I push social and emotional learning in the schools or healthy food in the schools, it's like, because those are our kids. They're all over. They're your kids. They're my kids. They're, you know, and my wife's a teacher, first grade. I go into her classroom. Those are all her kids. You know, we went to a football game. When I first met her, we went to a football game, high school football game, and all the kids that she had taught over the years were there. And there's like five deep circle around my wife. You know, and these kids are, she taught them in fourth grade. Now they're in high school. And they're, you know, Mrs. Ryan, Mrs. Gentile, was her main name, Mrs. Gentile, Mrs. Because we had just, you start dating, they're",
